      "description": "DKIM keys below 2048 bits are published at k1._domainkey.bookmyshow.com (1024 bits) and mandrill._domainkey.bookmyshow.com (1024 bits). Each meets the RFC 8301 minimum of 1024 bits, below the recommended 2048. The domain also publishes k2._domainkey.bookmyshow.com, s1._domainkey.bookmyshow.com, s2._domainkey.bookmyshow.com. We cannot tell from DNS which selector it signs its mail with. RFC 6376 section 6.1.2 has a verifier fetch the key named by the signature's s= tag, so a signature may name any selector the domain publishes, the weaker one included.",
      "impact": "This is a margin, not a break: RFC 8301 permits the key, and recommends 2048 bits for signers. Moving to 2048 bits brings the domain in line with that recommendation.",
      "remediation": "If a weaker selector is no longer used for signing, remove its record. Otherwise generate a new 2048-bit key with your mail provider, publish it under a new selector, switch signing to it, and remove the old selector once no mail is signed with it.",
      "references": [
        "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8301"
      ],
